 { If the pawns are separated by more files, the situation becomes more interesting, assuming, of course, that there are more pawns on the board. Otherwise, it is almost impossible that the stronger side's king does not manage to secure promotion of one pawn.It is Black's turn. However, there is not a single square on the whole board from which the white king fails to support his pawns in time. Let us see a possible sequence of play: } 1... Kd5 2. a5 $1 { Preventing the capture of the central pawn } Kc6 3. Kg2 Kb5 4. Kf3 Kxa5 5. Ke4 Kb6 6. Kd5 Kc7 7. Ke6 $18 { The white king has reached one of the key squares of the d4 pawn. Therefore, the most important scenario arises with more pawns on the board. We will come back to this idea when we deal with the floating square (Ending 88) . } 
