 { If the passed pawn is on the central file nearest to the blocked rook's pawns, it is easier for the defending king to develop some counterplay against the pawns. Moreover, the stronger side's king has less room to attack the enemy rook's pawn. The approach to this ending is thus very different. Let us start with a position where the blocked pawn is well advanced. In contrast with the previous ending, this is less favourable to the stronger side. } 1. Kc1 Kd4 2. Kc2 Kc4 3. d3+ Kd4 { Immediate counterattack by } ( 3... Kb5 { draws easily, but we will follow this line to check that the result is the same, no matter how advanced the passed pawn is. } ) 
4. Kd2 Kd5 5. Kc3 Kc5 $1 { The only instance when the counterattack does not work and Black must keep the white king away from the key squares. } ( 5... Kc6 6. Kc4 $1 Kb6 7. Kd5 $1 Kxa6 8. Kc6 { trapping the black king and winning. We will study this in detail in the following positions. Other moves are losing, such } ) 
 ( 5... Kd6 6. Kd4 { and the d-pawn promotes. } ) 
6. d4+ Kd5 7. Kd3 Kd6 8. Kc4 Kc6 9. d5+ Kd6 10. Kd4 Kd7 11. Kc5 Kc7 12. d6+ Kd7 13. Kd5 Kd8 14. Kc6 Kc8 { This is the position we wanted to see. When the pawns are blocked on the 6th rank, the stronger side's king has no room to attack the enemy pawn and it is a draw. } 15. d7+ Kd8 $11 
